2:identities and re-acceptance procedure, and names this subsection as its\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:824:### Results\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:911:Availability, assessment and acceptance are separate results, each with its\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:916:fitness or acceptance, and acceptance is never inferred from a successful\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:919:[§5](../EVALUATION.md#5-acceptance)).\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:991:### Foundation to law: binding a project to the method\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1004:immutable released versions that a project selects, installs and later\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1015:| P6 (independent); P3 (independent); P4b (derived from P0 and P2, with P0's premise that step-produced facts are not re-acquirable); P1 (independent); P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise); P2 (independent); instance: released method | By P6 and P3 one law has one owner on one released basis, so the method has one authority surface, each law has one owning member, and the members govern only together, as one released version. By P4b a derived surface depends on its sources, so it preserves the owning clauses' conditions, carries their digests and is stale when one changes; by P1 a matching digest establishes identity, not fidelity. | [§11](../PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#11-one-constitutional-surface-and-version-boundary) | the generic staleness law, which the digest binding refines for the carriers of a cut or installation: [ACTIVATION §2 Staleness of derived surfaces](../ACTIVATION.md#staleness-of-derived-surfaces); which concrete forms the method accepts as interoperability boundaries: this member (§1, §4); any widening of Product scope to an executable tool, runtime or realisation: separate explicit human authorisation; a policy of this member: the members govern only together, as one released version | 1, 6, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1027:### Foundation to law\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1087:### Foundation to law: chain, outcome and build-tenant binding\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:1:# RELEASE\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:9:coordinated release-matched asset cohorts, acceptance, external\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:19:version), [EVALUATION](EVALUATION.md) (§1 claims, §5 acceptance),\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:25:[PRODUCT_DEFINITION §4](PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#4-shared-installed-release-basis-and-toolchain-manager)\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:26:(the installed release basis, the adoption plan and the toolchain manager);\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:32:## 1. Context and position\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:91:### Foundation to law: release and successor conservation\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:115:| P3 (independent); P6 (independent); instance: recursive products | By P3 each object binds its own identity and basis, so substrate, development product, source project, release cut, product, install, artifact and product definition are distinct objects. A mutable source workspace binds no fixed identity and so is not called a product; by P6 binding grants nothing, so an install acting as builder substrate is bound as a development product and transfers no meaning or authority to the product being authored, and a product definition is a source-project surface, not the released artifact. | [FOUNDATIONS §2 Product lineage objects](FOUNDATIONS.md#product-lineage-objects), the taxonomy and its governing rules | the eight roles, their concept identities and the compiler analogy as the intended model: choices of FOUNDATIONS §2; their application to lineage: [SPEC_METHOD §6](SPEC_METHOD.md#6-lineage-and-release) (family F10) | 1, 2 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:118:| P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise); P6 (independent) | By P5 a value decides only what its evidence establishes, and by P6 an operation's outcome is not its acceptance, so a refused, stopped or fail-closed release operation, an invalid state and a failed check report in the one core, and none is read as success. | [§1](#1-context-and-position), Results | none | 3, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:120:| P3 (independent); P6 (independent); P0 (recovered); P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise); instance: published cuts | By P3 review and replay need one fixed subject, so publication fixes one exact immutable subject, and a change to any qualifying byte, a release-scoped asset included, needs a higher immutable cut. By P6 and P0 consumers rely only on what was released, so mutable candidate source is non-operative for released consumers. | [§3](#3-phases), Mutable candidate construction and Immutable RC publication; [§6 Release-scoped assets](#release-scoped-assets) | the division into three phases (mutable candidate construction; immutable publication; exact-cut qualification, acceptance and explicit adoption), the rule that there is no second final cut, the ten publication steps, the six release-scoped surfaces and the RC ordinal: domain choices of this member | 1, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:121:| P6 (independent); P1 (independent); P3 (independent); instance: published cuts | By P3 and P6 a verdict holds for the exact subject and claim it binds, so qualification binds the exact cut, claims, basis, each claimed property with its observation boundary, evidence, verdicts, gaps and successor relation, and acceptance is the acceptance authority's verdict over the exact immutable cut. By P1 a weaker kind of evidence does not stand for a stronger claim. A verdict moves no selector, and a change to a qualifying byte leaves the verdict on the old cut. | [§3 Exact-cut qualification](#exact-cut-qualification); [§8 Acceptance criteria](#acceptance-criteria) | the seven acceptance criteria: domain choices of this member; the generic claim and acceptance law: [EVALUATION §1](EVALUATION.md#1-claims) and [§5](EVALUATION.md#5-acceptance) (family F6); acceptance of each cut: the human Product authority | 7, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:122:| P3 (independent); P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise); P6 (independent); instance: published cuts, mutable carriers | By P3 a selector names an existing cut and never governs, so it advances strictly by ordinal, only to an existing immutable cut, and is refused otherwise; it answers which highest cut exists, never which cut governs a consumer. By P6 a consumer's basis changes only by its own authority, so it changes only by an explicit adoption whose presented plan is accepted; by P5 drift invalidates that acceptance, and resolution fails closed. | [§3 Monotonic version-line advancement](#monotonic-version-line-advancement); [§4](#4-selector-and-consumer-adoption) | the seven advancement steps, the four refusal conditions and the channel URI as discovery selector: domain choices of this member; the adoption plan, its hashing and the manager commands: [PRODUCT_DEFINITION §4](PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#4-shared-installed-release-basis-and-toolchain-manager) (family F12) | 1, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:124:| P6 (independent); P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise); instance: external services | By P5 a skipped evaluation has no result, and by P6 a claim is qualified only on its evidence, so a claim that depends on an external service is qualified against a healthy dependency or narrowed before acceptance, and a skipped evaluation establishes nothing about the dependent capability. | [§8 External qualification dependencies](#external-qualification-dependencies) | none | 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:126:| P4b (derived from P0 and P2, with P0's premise that step-produced facts are not re-acquirable); P6 (independent); instance: successor lines | By P4b a change on a support line persists into what the successor retains, and by P6 its effect needs a disposition by evidence, so where a maintenance or support line may affect retained claims, the release basis names its watched source, observation boundary, inclusion policy and successor candidate, and relevant changes receive a disposition at integration and at qualification. | [§9](#9-successor-baseline-conservation-stdo-up-015), the support-line paragraph | the record, branch strategy, comparison and proof mechanism: the consumer; a policy of this member: the four items the release basis names (watched source, observation boundary, inclusion policy, successor candidate) | 1, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:138:- Row 2, release objects and release identities: [IDENTITY §9](IDENTITY.md#9-release-version-boundary), where the immutable cut is the only exact operative release identifier and the selector only names the highest-ordinal published cut; [SPEC_METHOD §6](SPEC_METHOD.md#6-lineage-and-release), where the mutable tag is a discovery selector and not a dependency, evidence or constitutional basis; [PRODUCT_DEFINITION §4](PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#4-shared-installed-release-basis-and-toolchain-manager), where the definition's channel selector takes its alias semantics from the release law and is not operative authority; [ACTIVATION §2](ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope), Acquisition, where a candidate source or a version-line selector is no substitute for the exact selected basis; the [ODD family table](ODD.md#foundation-to-law), whose row for builder, release and source distinctions imports F15 for the selected release and mutable source.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:139:- Row 3, position and triage: [ODD §15](ODD.md#15-release-boundary), where publication, selector advancement and acceptance stay a process surface apart from graph truth; [SPEC_METHOD §6](SPEC_METHOD.md#6-lineage-and-release), where the cut is release metadata and not part of the live project specification; [§3 Mutable candidate construction](#mutable-candidate-construction), where bounded fixes follow intake triage, and [Immutable RC publication](#immutable-rc-publication), step 1, which confirms the triaged scope.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:141:- Row 5, refs, subtree, conservation, naming and the Product subject: [§3 Immutable RC publication](#immutable-rc-publication), steps 6 and 7, which record the namespace, subtree root and tree and create the project-qualified tags; [§3 Exact-cut qualification](#exact-cut-qualification), which binds the namespace, qualified ref, tag object, commit, trees and declared member set; [§4](#4-selector-and-consumer-adoption), whose channel resolution enumerates a namespace's published tags, preserved historical cuts included; [§7](#7-coordinated-release-matched-asset-cohorts), whose cut names and refs render the cohort suffix and whose cut tags are create-only; otherwise no separate consumer applies: the ref shapes, namespaces and the Product subject are this member's domain choices, and the installed-manifest binding of PRODUCT_DEFINITION §4 names the cut's tag object, commit, tree and inventory without naming this section.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:142:- Row 6, candidate construction, immutable publication and release-scoped assets: [AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §8](AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#8-calculus-identity), whose edition identity record is issued at the release of its bytes and nowhere earlier; [§7](#7-coordinated-release-matched-asset-cohorts) and [Two-commit construction and one publication transaction](#two-commit-construction-and-one-publication-transaction), where derived members are regenerated and released under the new cut and the cut tags are published atomically or refused; [§8 Acceptance criteria](#acceptance-criteria), where release-scoped assets describe the exact cut; [§3 Exact-cut qualification](#exact-cut-qualification), where a qualifying-byte change leaves the verdict on the old cut and a higher cut is published.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:143:- Row 7, exact-cut qualification and acceptance criteria: [FOUNDATIONS §4 Change impact and evidence reuse](FOUNDATIONS.md#change-impact-and-evidence-reuse), where the acceptance criteria are the mandatory gates that stay applicable irrespective of the selection of checks; [§7 Qualification and recovery](#qualification-and-recovery), whose content, local-ref-graph and post-publication qualification bind the exact cut; [§8 External qualification dependencies](#external-qualification-dependencies), where a dependent claim is qualified or narrowed before acceptance; [§9](#9-successor-baseline-conservation-stdo-up-015), where an unresolved disposition blocks qualification of the affected claim.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:144:- Row 8, selector advancement and consumer adoption: [ODD §4](ODD.md#4-method-library-classes), where the selected immutable cut is consumer authority and mutable source and the moving selector cannot repair, replace or outrank it; [PRODUCT_DEFINITION §4](PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#4-shared-installed-release-basis-and-toolchain-manager), whose `adopt` resolves the selector only to present an exact digest-bound plan and whose `sync` never consults it; [SPEC_METHOD §6](SPEC_METHOD.md#6-lineage-and-release), where the selector cannot silently change a consumer selection.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:146:- Row 10, external qualification dependencies: no separate consumer applies. The rule acts inside this member's qualification and acceptance (§3 Exact-cut qualification, §8 Acceptance criteria), where a claim that depends on an external service is qualified against a healthy dependency or narrowed; the result of a clean skip is FOUNDATIONS §4 Results', Not performed, which this section applies, and no section of another member names this section.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:147:- Row 11, successor baseline conservation: [EVALUATION §5](EVALUATION.md#5-acceptance), where a successor's acceptance evidence includes the disposition record and an unresolved disposition blocks acceptance; [SPEC_METHOD §2 Requirement categories and classification](SPEC_METHOD.md#requirement-categories-and-classification), where each predecessor claim takes its disposition by this section's predicates and no lifecycle status supplies it, and [§7 Fundamental migration](SPEC_METHOD.md#fundamental-migration), where no migration class is or supplies a disposition; [FOUNDATIONS §1](FOUNDATIONS.md#1-vocabulary-of-standing), which keeps the standing of a commitment apart from a disposition, and [§4 Preservation through construction](FOUNDATIONS.md#preservation-through-construction), where a change to the governing meaning follows the successor rules; [TICKETS §7 Recorded judgment and owner-ruling continuity](TICKETS.md#recorded-judgment-and-owner-ruling-continuity) and [ODD §11.5D](ODD.md#115d-constructive-evaluation-and-yield-loop), which keep repricing dispositions and closure decisions apart from the disposition of a predecessor claim; [CONFORMANCE §5](CONFORMANCE.md#5-discriminator-coverage), discriminators 7, 8 and 49; [§2 Project-subtree release identity](#project-subtree-release-identity) and [§3 Exact-cut qualification](#exact-cut-qualification), which bind the dispositions and the successor relation this section requires; the selected work carrier records the disposition values and supplies no decision by its presence. Deterministic-kind migration is consumed by [AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §5](AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#5-traversals-and-functor-kinds) and [§7](AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#7-layers), [ODD §5](ODD.md#probabilistic-compute) and [§11.7A](ODD.md#117a-conforming-agentic-development), whose unresolved admission-gate eligibility stays open under the migration relation, [TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE kind binding](TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE.md#closed-constraint-judgment-residual-and-stop-kinds), [DESIGN_MODULE §2](DESIGN_MODULE.md#2-graph-execution-alignment-and-escalation), [EVALUATION §2](EVALUATION.md#2-separation), and [CONFORMANCE D301-08](CONFORMANCE.md#deterministic-kind-and-continuation-discriminators).\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:153:## 2. Release identities\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:179:### Project-qualified Git refs\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:214:### Project-subtree release identity\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:243:### Historical ref and public-URI conservation\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:267:## 3. Phases\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:273:3. exact-cut qualification, acceptance and explicit consumer adoption.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:277:selector; acceptance remains a verdict over the exact immutable RC rather\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:282:### Mutable candidate construction\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:297:### Immutable RC publication\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:300:and possible acceptance. To publish it, the project (P3, P5, P6):\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:328:### Exact-cut qualification\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:342:installed behaviour; packaging equivalence does not prove semantic\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:350:advertises the highest published RC; acceptance does not move it, rewrite\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:353:### Monotonic version-line advancement\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:382:## 4. Selector and consumer adoption\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:395:`stdo://releases/v<version>-rc.<n>/` URI and installed-manifest digest,\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:396:the installed release basis of PRODUCT_DEFINITION §4. Moving the Git\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:398:explicit Product-Definition transition that resolves, presents, installs,\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:418:object, peeled commit, tree and installed-manifest digest, then emits a\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:420:operator's acceptance and re-derives it before changing any Product\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:422:acceptance. A fleet uses the same law with one aggregate digest over all\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:432:## 5. Naming\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:455:## 6. Release-scoped assets and the Product subject\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:457:### Release-scoped assets\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:462:- release and installed-distribution manifests;\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:464:- package or installed-carrier metadata;\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:479:### Product subject and repository carrier\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:497:## 7. Coordinated release-matched asset cohorts\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:513:inventories, tags, acceptance judgments or release authorities (P3, P6).\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:537:### Two-commit construction and one publication transaction\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:539:Exact installed-manifest identity includes the annotated STDO tag object,\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:548:3. that exact local cut is installed and verified, producing the manifest\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:583:### Qualification and recovery\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:609:## 8. Acceptance\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:611:### Acceptance criteria\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:628:retained evidence satisfies it only under the validity conditions stated\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:635:the generic acceptance law).\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:637:### External qualification dependencies\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:649:healthy dependency or narrowed before acceptance (P6, P5).\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:651:## 9. Successor baseline conservation (STDO-UP-015)\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:663:Evidence for a disposition is retained or reassessed under\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:693:acceptance result. Qualification evaluates each\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:704:Where a maintenance or support line may affect retained claims, the\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:711:[EVALUATION §5](EVALUATION.md#5-acceptance) states what acceptance\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:715:### Deterministic-kind migration\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:764:policy and waives no acceptance gate.\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/RELEASE.md:766:## 10. Open questions\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
