{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"/opt/homebrew/Cellar/python@3.13/3.13.7/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.13/bin/python3.13 -B /Users/jim/src/apps/specification_methodology-skills/a_c/build_tenants/graphify/code/ac_support.py --policy /private/var/folders/rz/r6wxvr0n15d906k2s0jw8j2h0000gn/T/stdo-skills-deployment-vup0_dwe/codex/control/worker-policy.json --policy-sha256 28f813256f7ec1fe45761de15364df01e52f5ad8f38e32048e580c92e5316463 --principal host call --request - <<'STDO_REQUEST'\n{\\\"schema\\\":\\\"a_c.fd-support.request/1\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"deploy-read-release-001\\\",\\\"operation\\\":\\\"source.read\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"source\\\":{\\\"engine\\\":\\\"workspace-regular-file\\\",\\\"root\\\":\\\"method\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"standards/RELEASE.md\\\",\\\"object\\\":null,\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"1ec805a4acb60f7ce0d061cf4ba6656690500e27ae6756866eaafa34aad071e0\\\",\\\"selector\\\":{\\\"lines\\\":[267,351]}},\\\"encoding\\\":\\\"utf8\\\"}}\nSTDO_REQUEST\"","aggregated_output":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:d485e71a4f70bf2ec3bb735eea2313d160be8d81fc42988f6cb0b97fb4fff0af\",\"sha256\":\"d485e71a4f70bf2ec3bb735eea2313d160be8d81fc42988f6cb0b97fb4fff0af\"},\"id\":\"deploy-read-release-001\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=b01c93a02aca57f953d165d47e32992a50b91d9f57cf292dec69c77f596f40bc]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"92fe2f3c09a59b4e9a732463a41d4a45d7efd693648159621e03c75f7127eac5\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 3. Phases\\n\\nThe release process has three phases (P0, P3, P6):\\n\\n1. mutable candidate construction;\\n2. immutable RC publication and monotonic version-line advancement; and\\n3. exact-cut qualification, acceptance and explicit consumer adoption.\\n\\nThere is no second final cut after an accepted RC. Publication immediately\\nmakes the new highest ordinal discoverable through the version-line\\nselector; acceptance remains a verdict over the exact immutable RC rather\\nthan a second carrier. If any byte capable of affecting the Product or a\\nrelease-scoped claim changes, the project publishes and reviews a higher\\nimmutable RC cut instead (P3, P6).\\n\\n### Mutable candidate construction\\n\\nDuring candidate construction (P0, P6):\\n\\n- bounded fixes are allowed after intake triage;\\n- qualification and operator review may continue;\\n- release notes may remain draft until RC publication;\\n- the RC branch may move; and\\n- mutable source is explicitly non-operative for released consumers.\\n\\nThe intended Product boundary, release-scoped claims, excluded\\nsource-project state and qualification basis become explicit before RC\\npublication. A material scope change is repriced instead of being silently\\nabsorbed (P2, P6).\\n\\n### Immutable RC publication\\n\\nA published RC cut exists to provide one exact subject for review, replay\\nand possible acceptance. To publish it, the project (P3, P5, P6):\\n\\n1. confirms the triaged scope and lawful constitutional re-entry;\\n2. declares the exact Product release subject and release-scoped claim\\n   set;\\n3. reconciles the release note and other release-facing assets to that\\n   subject;\\n4. runs the proportionate pre-RC qualification and operator checks;\\n5. commits the exact carrier;\\n6. resolves and records the exact Project Release Namespace, Project\\n   Subtree root and Project Subtree tree;\\n7. creates one annotated immutable `<project>/v<version>-rc.<n>` tag and\\n   an annotated `<project>/v<version>` selector tag over that same commit\\n   in a shared release source;\\n8. atomically pushes the carrier, RC branch, immutable RC tag, selector\\n   and optional release branch where the transport supports it;\\n9. verifies remotely that the immutable tag, selector and release branch\\n   resolve to the highest published RC commit; and\\n10. records enough ref, subtree, identity and inventory evidence to\\n    reacquire the cut without trusting a mutable checkout.\\n\\nEach published RC tag is immutable. Further work increments `<n>` on the\\nsame version line when the intended line remains applicable. It does not\\nrequire a minor-version increment merely because review found a bounded\\nrepair. A published higher RC without matching selector advancement is an\\ninvalid, fail-closed release state; it never authorises resolution of the\\nolder cut as the version-line latest (P3, P5).\\n\\n### Exact-cut qualification\\n\\nQualification binds (P6, P3):\\n\\n- the Project Release Namespace, qualified immutable RC ref, annotated tag\\n  object, peeled commit, repository tree, Project Subtree root and tree,\\n  and declared Product member set;\\n- the exact release-scoped claim bytes;\\n- the governing Product and release basis;\\n- each claimed property and its observation boundary;\\n- evidence subjects, owner verdicts and unresolved gaps; and\\n- the complete successor relation to the declared predecessor (§9).\\n\\nEvidence matches the claim. Source success does not prove packaged or\\ninstalled behaviour; packaging equivalence does not prove semantic\\nbehaviour; artifact presence does not prove usability unless presence is\\nthe exact claim (P6, P1; EVALUATION §1 is the generic claim law).\\n\\nAn RC is accepted only when the required exact-cut review and operator\\nchecks are complete and the human authority accepts that exact subject. If\\nreview requires any qualifying-byte change, the verdict remains attached\\nto the old RC and a higher RC is published. The selector already\\nadvertises the highest published RC; acceptance does not move it, rewrite\\nit or create another final carrier (P6, P3).\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[267,351]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"1ec805a4acb60f7ce0d061cf4ba6656690500e27ae6756866eaafa34aad071e0\",\"selected_sha256\":\"faa886043022fd45985af90d9d560790cafdf16a924e505a556a447eac7c3323\",\"size_bytes\":3984,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"standards/RELEASE.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[267,351]},\"sha256\":\"1ec805a4acb60f7ce0d061cf4ba6656690500e27ae6756866eaafa34aad071e0\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"/opt/homebrew/Cellar/python@3.13/3.13.7/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.13/bin/python3.13 -B /Users/jim/src/apps/specification_methodology-skills/a_c/build_tenants/graphify/code/ac_support.py --policy /private/var/folders/rz/r6wxvr0n15d906k2s0jw8j2h0000gn/T/stdo-skills-deployment-vup0_dwe/codex/control/worker-policy.json --policy-sha256 28f813256f7ec1fe45761de15364df01e52f5ad8f38e32048e580c92e5316463 --principal host call --request - <<'STDO_REQUEST'\n{\\\"schema\\\":\\\"a_c.fd-support.request/1\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"deploy-read-identity-001\\\",\\\"operation\\\":\\\"source.read\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"source\\\":{\\\"engine\\\":\\\"workspace-regular-file\\\",\\\"root\\\":\\\"method\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"standards/IDENTITY.md\\\",\\\"object\\\":null,\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"2e7026aeec957ebb9b1aa0b01ae76b81b4236f02db93b040f158eadf31f5e08b\\\",\\\"selector\\\":{\\\"lines\\\":[97,166]}},\\\"encoding\\\":\\\"utf8\\\"}}\nSTDO_REQUEST\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"/opt/homebrew/Cellar/python@3.13/3.13.7/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.13/bin/python3.13 -B /Users/jim/src/apps/specification_methodology-skills/a_c/build_tenants/graphify/code/ac_support.py --policy /private/var/folders/rz/r6wxvr0n15d906k2s0jw8j2h0000gn/T/stdo-skills-deployment-vup0_dwe/codex/control/worker-policy.json --policy-sha256 28f813256f7ec1fe45761de15364df01e52f5ad8f38e32048e580c92e5316463 --principal host call --request - <<'STDO_REQUEST'\n{\\\"schema\\\":\\\"a_c.fd-support.request/1\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"deploy-read-identity-001\\\",\\\"operation\\\":\\\"source.read\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"source\\\":{\\\"engine\\\":\\\"workspace-regular-file\\\",\\\"root\\\":\\\"method\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"standards/IDENTITY.md\\\",\\\"object\\\":null,\\\"sha256\\\":\\\"2e7026aeec957ebb9b1aa0b01ae76b81b4236f02db93b040f158eadf31f5e08b\\\",\\\"selector\\\":{\\\"lines\\\":[97,166]}},\\\"encoding\\\":\\\"utf8\\\"}}\nSTDO_REQUEST\"","aggregated_output":"{\"evidence\":{\"id\":\"sha256:1547c1ca9e7b43d6349384aead03a2c4b40214566d0520173f96685e36eb7af3\",\"sha256\":\"1547c1ca9e7b43d6349384aead03a2c4b40214566d0520173f96685e36eb7af3\"},\"id\":\"deploy-read-identity-001\",\"operation\":\"source.read\",\"operation_outcome\":{\"cause\":\"declared output available\",\"core_value\":\"satisfied\",\"evidence\":[],\"limits\":[\"output availability only; no semantic acceptance\"],\"predicate\":\"B(source.read)[request_sha256=8761ac96432913e35e2b52425cddb101d5704f4608da80c2545576b9b3272dba]: declared output availability\",\"source_parent\":{\"clause\":\"results\",\"member\":\"FOUNDATIONS.md\",\"sha256\":\"92fe2f3c09a59b4e9a732463a41d4a45d7efd693648159621e03c75f7127eac5\"},\"value\":\"returned\"},\"refusal\":null,\"result\":{\"artifact\":null,\"value\":{\"acquisition\":{},\"content\":\"## 2. Core law\\n\\n### Scoped identity\\n\\nIdentity is always scoped. At minimum, identity should be interpretable\\nthrough authority, kind and value. So the minimum semantic model is:\\n\\n```text\\nidentity = authority + kind + value\\n```\\n\\nThis is the scoped identity of an Object (FOUNDATIONS §2). Optional\\nadditional coordinates may include parent scope, revision, publication\\nversion and temporal coordinates (P3).\\n\\n### Uniqueness\\n\\nIdentity is unique within its lawful scope. The requirement is not that\\nevery identity looks globally unique as a naked string. The requirement is\\n(P3):\\n\\n- no two live objects of the same kind may share the same identity within\\n  the same authority scope;\\n- aliasing across scopes remains explicit; and\\n- serialisation preserves enough hierarchy to prevent accidental\\n  collision.\\n\\n### Type\\n\\nIdentity is typed. Different kinds of thing should not be collapsed into\\none undifferentiated identity space. Examples of distinct identity kinds\\ninclude goal, intent, requirement, requirement_family,\\nacceptance_criterion, scenario, design_decision, published_function,\\ncarrier_surface, release, install, run, function_call, continuation and\\nevent. The method does not treat a family identity as if it were a\\nconcrete requirement identity, or a release version as if it were object\\nidentity (P3).\\n\\n### Stability\\n\\nIdentity should remain stable for the governed lifecycle it serves. This\\ndoes not mean every identity is immutable forever. It means identity is\\nstable enough to support traceability, comparison, replay, publication\\nand supersession. If an identity changes, the method should make the\\nrelationship explicit rather than silently reusing the old identity for a\\ndifferent object (P3, P4b).\\n\\n### Boundaries and sufficiency claims\\n\\nAn object's boundary, what belongs to the object and what its identity\\ndoes not depend on, is a claim with a proof obligation\\n([FOUNDATIONS §3](FOUNDATIONS.md#3-commitments), P3), not a property that\\nits storage, schema or name establishes. A boundary claim names its\\nobject, the evidence that supports it and the basis it holds on, and it\\nholds only as far as that evidence goes (P3, P6). A claim that an\\nobject's observed boundary state is sufficient for a task is a material\\nsufficiency claim of\\n[RF-003](REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-003-material-sufficiency), bounded by\\nthat task: it is not completeness, and it does not establish that the\\nboundary screens the object's interior from everything outside it. That\\nstronger claim, a statistical Markov boundary, is a hypothesis\\n([FOUNDATIONS §5](FOUNDATIONS.md#5-hypotheses)); no work relies on it as\\nestablished, and a profile that uses such a construct states its\\nepistemic status (P5). Purpose and material relation: this law was\\ndecided only in the optional world-model profile, so a Product that does\\nnot adopt that profile had no rule for boundary claims, and the common\\nsingle ownership requirement, item 8, requires generic law to be\\navailable without an optional profile; [WORLD_MODEL §4](WORLD_MODEL.md#4-the-markov-object-construct)\\nrefines it.\\n\\n\",\"coordinates\":{\"lines\":[97,166]},\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"parent_sha256\":\"2e7026aeec957ebb9b1aa0b01ae76b81b4236f02db93b040f158eadf31f5e08b\",\"selected_sha256\":\"2a1a4e7b656c96d076beceefec04e7f050b6276395642833c1ed52dc9188f133\",\"size_bytes\":3061,\"source\":{\"engine\":\"workspace-regular-file\",\"object\":null,\"path\":\"standards/IDENTITY.md\",\"root\":\"method\",\"selector\":{\"lines\":[97,166]},\"sha256\":\"2e7026aeec957ebb9b1aa0b01ae76b81b4236f02db93b040f158eadf31f5e08b\"}}},\"schema\":\"a_c.fd-support.response/1\",\"status\":\"ok\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
